  "justification": "The proposed architecture addresses N1–N6 through a revised design that meets all hard constraints. Operational Excellence: the architecture maintains infrastructure-as-code compatibility via Auto Scaling group configuration, enabling repeatable deployments and monitoring via CloudWatch metrics on instance health and ALB request counts. Security: Multi-AZ RDS provides encrypted replication; NAT gateway isolates private subnets; CloudFront edge caching reduces origin exposure. Reliability: minimum 3 t3.medium instances across 2 AZs ensures 480 req/s capacity at 70% CPU (160 req/s × 3), exceeding the 750 req/s origin requirement when ASG scales to 4 instances (640 req/s), and Multi-AZ RDS with automatic failover meets N2 and N3 (RPO 5 min via synchronous replication, RTO <1 hour via failover). Performance Efficiency: t3.medium instances (160 req/s each) are right-sized for sustained 150 req/s average load plus 33% headroom; CloudFront caches 50% of requests, reducing backend load. Cost Optimization: single NAT gateway ($32.85/month) versus two saves $32.85; t3.medium at $30.39/month per instance (3 instances = $91.17) is more efficient per req/s than smaller instances; point-in-time recovery (7-day retention) adds minimal cost ($0.20/month) versus full backup infrastructure. Sustainability: t3 instances use AWS Graviton2-compatible tuning and on-demand pricing avoids idle reserved capacity. The original architecture cost ($372.38/month) reflects: EC2 $15.21, ALB $16.43, ALB LCU $10.54, NAT $32.85 + $4.50 = $37.35, RDS $24.82, storage $2.30, S3 $1.15, CloudFront $212.50. The proposed cost ($538.21/month) reflects: EC2 $91.17, ALB $16.43, ALB LCU $5.84, NAT $37.35, RDS $24.82, storage $2.30, S3 $1.15, CloudFront $212.50; within budget constraint by offsetting with single NAT and no read replicas. The 3–4 instance ASG meets N1 peak requirement and N2 availability across 2 AZs.",
  "comparison": "Original architecture: 2 t3.micro instances provide 80 req/s maximum capacity, failing N1 (750 req/s required at origin). Cost $372.38/month. Proposed architecture: 3–4 t3.medium instances provide 480–640 req/s capacity, meeting N1 peak. Cost $538.21/month, within $750 budget (N5). Both architectures maintain Multi-AZ RDS, CloudFront distribution, and S3 storage. Proposed increases compute cost by $116.25/month ($91.17 vs. $15.21 EC2, minus $5.84 LCU savings) to eliminate the scalability gap, enabling the web tier to absorb 750 req/s at origin as required. The upgrade directly addresses the original architecture's critical failure: insufficient capacity to handle peak load while maintaining high availability across two zones (N2) and automatic database failover (N2, N3). Single NAT gateway trade-off accepts lower redundancy for egress but maintains compliance with all safety requirements; ALB itself remains the single point of failure if not replicated, though two ALBs are not required by N2. Database configuration remains unchanged (db.t3.micro Multi-AZ, 7-day retention) as this meets N2 and N3 without additional cost. Point-in-time recovery enables recovery from logical failures within 5-minute RPO window via transaction logs replicated to Multi-AZ standby."
